{
  "dom_id": "step-q109828",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Given that the $5$th term of a geometric sequence is $-125$, and the common ratio is $5,$ find the formula for the $n$th term.",
  "worked_solution": "The common ratio is $r=5,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=-125,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{5-1} \\\\ -125 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{4} \\\\ -{5}^{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{5}^{n-1}}{5} \\\\  & =-{5}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
